    <td valign="top" width="618" height="164"> 
      <p class="Heading">History</p>
      <p>A long time ago in another realm, and another time a strange young high 
        elf heard a calling. It was a strange calling. I was not a sound, it was 
        a feeling. He was an outcast of his society, wild and some said crazy 
        and Elves don’t go for the wild and crazy types, especially high elves. 
        As a result he was shunned in most establishments. Few would talk to him 
        and none would name him friend.</p>
      <p>So, having nothing to keep him in him in his home land Ziff decided he 
        would follow this calling and see where it took him. As a mere level 2 
        apprentice he packed his meager belongings donned his tattered cloak and 
        headed out across the forest.</p>
      <p>He traveled for many months. Following this feeling took him across his 
        small continent to its far shores where he met dwarves and saw the ocean 
        for the first time. But he was still pulled onwards. So he scraped together 
        enough coin to barter a ship ride across the ocean to a new land. And 
        onward he went, passing places he had only heard of in legends. He passed 
        through the wilds of Kthicor, the keep at High Hold and the plains of 
        Karana until he arrived at the gates Queynos. It was here that the calling 
        seemed to be drawing him. Tattered, starving and frightened to the point 
        of babbling madness he stumbled into the great city.</p>
      <p>When Ziff arrived in Qeynos he found a strange land and a strange people. 
        He was now deep in the land of Humans, Halflings and Erudites. Elves were 
        mostly a rumor to these people and so Ziff tolerated both the curiosities 
        of the children as well as the cold shoulders of the adults. In a strange 
        land, following a strange calling he was all alone.</p>
      <p>And so Ziff, in his loneliness and confusion, began frequenting taverns 
        where he performed minor enchantments and tricks for his meals. There 
        he discovered a foul brew that he was sure his ancestors would have hated 
        but somehow… he fell in love with. It was dark, strong and tasted like 
        the dirty hole he felt like he had fallen into. So, long into the nights 
        he would sit in bars and drink scotch and tell those who would listen 
        tales about his home land and his travels.</p>
      <p>It was during one of these frequent evenings that he met a halfing named 
        Fibben. Fibben was a loud, crass wanderer who, upon meeting Ziff, immediately 
        tried to get him to switch from Scotch to Fibben’s home brewed fish wine. 
        The simple smell of the stuff nearly made Ziff retch and the little Halfling 
        reeked of it. It seems Fibben spent most of his time either making the 
        stuff or drinking it. But he was a jolly sort and he and Ziff soon created 
        a sort of bond that only a couple of drunken strangers can truly appreciate.</p>
      <p>A week or two later a haughty Paladin and his quiet Clerical side kick 
        were sitting in the Inn eating dinner. Due to some mumblings about the 
        horrid fishy smell, a conversation was struck between the four. The Paladin 
        was recently in from Freeport and was seeking new adventure. The cleric 
        had recently stepped off the Erudite island of Odus and was also drawn 
        by some strange calling. As it turns out, they also soon bonded with Ziff 
        and Fibben, because this strange wanderlust seemed to bring them together 
        and create a common bond. Though neither Ziff or Fibben could ever get 
        Wildride to take a drink, he would still often join them at the bar to 
        discuss his adventures as he crossed the continent from Freeport. Faan 
        usually sat quietly reading scripture and listening to his new friends, 
        but his presence was just as commonplace.</p>
      <p>In the following weeks a woman named Luifang joined the group. Luifang 
        was a monk who was studying in Qeynos. Luifang was nearly as loud as Fibben 
        in her jests and conversation. The meeting was again, brought along by 
        chance as she was sitting at the bar and fainted. (Later it was discovered, 
        Luifang faints a lot) The four rushed to assist her and within minutes 
        a bond was formed.</p>
      <p>A few days later Luifang brought one of her brethren to the groups evening 
        activities, another monk named Krug. Krug was one of the quietest of the 
        bunch but had some interesting tales to tell and could somehow stomach 
        Fibben’s fish wine, and so he too was included into the gang.</p>
      <p>As the weeks wore and the group began getting tighter the locals started 
        calling them “those bastards”. To some this was a comment on their common 
        lack of demonstratable lineage. To others it was a comment about their 
        loud boisterous behavior. Not to mention the smell that followed the little 
        Halfling around. In either event, it was not a compliment, but the name 
        started to spread and soon when they walked into any bar whispers could 
        be heard “those bastards”.</p>
      <p>It didn’t take long for the boisterous crew to turn the tables on the 
        insult. Soon they wandered into bars and shouted loudly “Bastards are 
        Home!” or “Here come the Bastards” and even “House full of Bastards!”. 
        At the same time they decided to rent a small home together and this became 
        known simply as the “House of Bastards”. They had a name, they had a reputation 
        and a headquarters. All they lacked was respect.</p>
      <p>One late night, in a fit of insanity and drunkenness, Ziff made an announcement. 
        He was sick of people looking down on them. He was sick of being told 
        he was a useless mad man with a bunch of looser friends. Mostly, he was 
        drunk and sick of listening to a group of young adventurers brag about 
        the gnolls they had recently met in the hills of Qeynos and killed. So, 
        Ziff made an announcement. He announced that he and the rest of the Bastards 
        were going to head out tomorrow and find the home of these gnolls. He 
        announced that he and the rest of the “Bastards” were going to take the 
        fight straight to the gnolls and do something really nuts. They were going 
        to attack the stronghold itself. In one drunken, brazen, crazed moment 
        the destiny of the group was forever altered and “House of Bastards” was 
        truly born.</p>
      <p>Ziff and his friends were true to their word. They brought death and 
        destruction to the gnolls. They fought their way into the clans homes 
        and just made a running slaughter, brining terror and death to the torn 
        ear clan. As brazen adventurers they dared to not only kill them, but 
        scalped most of them to take back evidence of their work.</p>
      <p>Again and again they went back, returning every night with piles of trashy 
        trinkets and stacks of gnoll scalps. The Qeynos guards laughed at them 
        at first, then grew quiet after they consistently returned, burdened to 
        the point of nearly crawling. And every night they would come through 
        the gates shouting “The House of Bastards have returned!”</p>
      <p>As they worked together they refined their skills and gained new ones. 
        They became a finely honed machine with a leader tottering on the brink 
        of insanity. The groups reputation spread quickly due to not just to their 
        continued success, but their boisterous advertisement of their name and 
        their barroom stories. Their tales were outlandish and brazen. The stories 
        spread to merchants and commoners. The name “House of Bastards” had gained 
        both respect and renown.</p>
      <p>As a group, they leveraged their new found local fame by taking on mercenary 
        jobs, clearing islands of skeletons and zombies, attacking bandit camps, 
        anything that was profitable and interesting.</p>
      <p>Eventually the group decided to venture out and establish a new base 
        in the far off lands of Freeport. There they met new friends who joined 
        the group. Lorsua, a lonely druid joined the company as well as Zoritsa, 
        a dark elven enchanter.</p>
      <p>It was during this time that a Ranger by the name Kalladim happened across 
        the group. He had heard a bit about their adventures and was curious to 
        meet the band of brave fools. Kalladim was the leader of a noble adventurers 
        guild named Oracle of Virtue. Kalladim was a knowledgeable leader of great 
        swordsmanship. He had battled dragons. He had killed giants and had made 
        forays into the very Plane of Fear.</p>
      <p>His guild was made up of characters with similar experiences and skills. 
        It was a small guild with noble ambitions and quiet honor. The House of 
        Bastards, now commonly referred to as HoB, were welcomed into the guild 
        hall as friends, but not as members. To gain membership to the guild they 
        would need to demonstrate their skill and teamwork and so, late one night 
        while drinking scotch by the fire Kalladim shared a tale with the Bastards. 
        What follows is that tale.</p>
      <p>Greetings HoB members:</p>
      <p>Your mission, should you choose to accept it, is to exact revenge on 
        the goblin hoards of Norrath, and return various items to prove your endeavors. 
        A bit of history should serve to enlighten you as to the reasons why:</p>
      <p>Long ago, when the goblins clans fought as one, there was a young elven 
        scout named Kiban Felewithe. Kiban had a natural gift in the realms of 
        stealth and shared the hatred his brother elves had for all goblins. The 
        goblin hoard was marching through the lands of Norrath, destroying all 
        that stood before them, and the Elven Nations joined forces to make a 
        final stand against them. Although Kiban was very young, he was given 
        a critical assignment: infiltrate the enemy camp and return with information 
        on their next target, so the elven forces could prepare an ambush and 
        crush their foes. He succeeded in his mission, but although the information 
        returned to the Elven capitol, Kiban did not, and had been captured.</p>
      <p>When the goblins moved to attack, the elves were ready and waiting. The 
        rampaging tide of the goblin army was laid asunder by arrow and fire, 
        and the fields were soon covered in blood. The defeat of the goblin army 
        shattered their moral, and the goblins scattered into the wind, but not 
        before they learned of Kiban's treachery. He was tortured beyond belief, 
        disemboweled, dismembered, and the remains taken back to various goblin 
        strongholds as trophies of war. Kiban was declared a hero to his people, 
        but his injustice was never properly avenged. If you have not recognized 
        the name, look to our history and our founder Sylvain Felewithe, for YOU 
        will be the instrument to exact the revenge we have wanted for over a 
        millennia to obtain.</p>
      <p>The targets are as follows:</p>
      <p>Deep in the icy lands of the Barbarians, there is an ice goblin stronghold. 
        One of the various leaders of this clan, referred to by his people as 
        &quot;da Preechir&quot; carries a weapon forged of cold iron. This was 
        the tool of endless hours of torture for Kiban, and it must be purified 
        and destroyed at all costs. Return his weapon to me to any means you feel 
        necessary.</p>
      <p>Through the Beholder's Maze lies the heart of the Runnyeye goblin clan. 
        Their warlord is the direct ancestor of Kiban's torturer. We cannot tolerate 
        his existence and will wipe his seed from the realm of Norrath forever. 
        Exact Kiban's revenge by slaying him, preferable as brutally as possible, 
        and return to me his personal medallion crafted of the blackest iron as 
        proof of his demise.</p>
      <p>To the North of the Commons, in the heart of the Land of Lava, are the 
        goblins of Soluesk's eye. Therein lies a goblin foreman who oversees the 
        slaves of his clan. He wears an ancient tunic, rumored to have been crafted 
        of human skin and cured with elven blood. Our information leads us to 
        believe that blood was Kiban's very own. Return this abomination to me 
        so that Kiban's soul can leave this realm and know peace at last.</p>
      <p>Finally, near the land of elves is a great lake known as Dagnor's. The 
        aquatic goblins of old live there. One of the Tidal Lords carries a watery 
        ring, rumored to have been crafted by the magical forging of thousands 
        of elven tears. This ring is to be returned home and kept in the Grand 
        Hall as a tribute to all the elves that died on the field of battle so 
        long ago.</p>
      <p>Your HoB team has been created and assembled to serve as an elite strike 
        force in the name of Virtue and Honor. Now it is time to prove your worth, 
        and earn the rank of &quot;Claviger&quot;. Upon successful completion 
        of your quests, you will be welcomed into the Oracle as brothers and sisters. 
        I wish you Godspeed, and the best of luck.<br>
      </p>
      <p>More soon...</p>
